# How Do I Use Clover as a Living Mulch and Cover Crop?

## Short Answer

Clover is one of the most versatile and low-maintenance plants you can grow in a garden or farm setting.
It fixes atmospheric nitrogen into the soil through a symbiotic relationship with [[nitrogen-fixing-bacteria-and-biological-nitrogen-fixation|rhizobia bacteria]], reducing or eliminating the need for synthetic nitrogen fertilizers.
As a living mulch, it suppresses weeds, retains soil moisture, and provides habitat for beneficial insects while improving soil structure over time.

## Clover Species and Varieties

The most commonly used clover species each have distinct advantages depending on your climate and purpose.
[[white-clover-living-mulch|White clover (Trifolium repens)]] is a low-growing perennial that spreads by stolons, making it ideal for pathways, orchard floors, and between garden beds.
[[red-clover|Red clover (Trifolium pratense)]] grows taller and produces more biomass, making it better suited for cutting as green manure or hay.
Crimson clover (Trifolium incarnatum) is an annual that excels in cool-season cover cropping and attracts large numbers of pollinators with its bright red flowers.
Subterranean clover (Trifolium subterraneum) buries its own seed heads underground, making it an excellent self-reseeding option for Mediterranean and mild-winter climates.

